// company's `asset` number range of the fiscal year its date falls in (the company's date today in its
// configured time zone; 422 `config.no_number_range` without one). It takes its class's depreciation
// terms in every ledger the company keeps that the class has terms in, with a residual value of zero
// and no depreciation start (depreciation then starts at the capitalisation date); change them with
// `PATCH`. Its centers are its first assignment, in force from creation: a cost center (optional) must
// be one of the company's that its class's depreciation account may take, valid and not locked for
// costs today (422 `ledger.cost_center_invalid`), and gives its profit center today (a different
// `profit_center_id` is 422 `ledger.profit_center_mismatch`); without a cost center the profit center
// is as given (optional; 422 `ledger.profit_center_invalid` for one that cannot be posted today). An
// asset whose depreciation account needs a cost center (a primary cost account while cost center
// accounting applies) without one and without the account's default cost center in the company is 422
// `assets.cost_center_required`, so its depreciation can always post. A legal entity the caller cannot
// act in is 404. Audited, and published as AssetChanged (`created`).
//
// POST /v1/assets
func (UnimplementedHandler) CreateAsset(ctx context.Context, req *AssetCreate, params CreateAssetParams) (r CreateAssetRes, _ error) {
	return r, ht.ErrNotImplemented
}

// CreateAssetAcquisition implements createAssetAcquisition operation.
//
// Capitalises onto an asset a posted journal line on an asset clearing account (`source`; ADR-0057
// §6, T57c; SAP's acquisition with automatic offsetting entry) or, by manual entry, an amount against
// an offset account (`offset_account_id`, T57d); one of the two is given (422
// `request.validation_failed` on `source` for both or neither). From a line: a supplier invoice item,
// a goods receipt or a manual entry posted on the clearing account of the asset's class in the asset's
// company. The line is named by its leading-ledger entry and line number
// (`GET /v1/asset-clearing-items` lists them); one that is not such a line is 422
// `assets.not_clearing_line`, a line of a reversed entry 422 `assets.source_reversed`, and a line
// whose entry has no copy in every ledger the company keeps (a ledger-specific entry) 422
// `assets.source_not_in_every_ledger`. The line of a reversal, reversing a line L, takes cost off
// again: only onto an asset holding posted acquisitions from L (422 `assets.source_reversed` onto any
// other asset, and for the reversal of a line nothing was capitalised from), each asset taking from it
// at most its posted acquisitions from L less what it already took from the reversal's line (422
// `assets.amount_exceeds_line` above that; the worklist offers each asset that bound, all of it by
// default). So a supplier invoice reversed in payables after its line was capitalised is corrected on
// the asset when the acquisition can no longer be reversed (409 `assets.later_movements` on its
// `:reverse`); once taken, the asset's acquisitions from L are not reversed until this one is (409
// `assets.reversal_line_capitalised`). `amount` is in the line's currency, all that is left of the
// line by default; it has the line's sign (a credit line, a supplier credit memo, is capitalised as a
// negative acquisition, which reduces cost) and is at most what is left (422
// `assets.amount_exceeds_line`); a negative acquisition that would leave the asset's cost below zero
// in a ledger, or one onto an asset holding no cost, is 422 `assets.cost_negative`. One line may be
// split across several assets, and an asset acquired by several acquisitions. Its local and group
// amounts are the line's in proportion, rounded half away from zero, the acquisition that uses the
// rest of the line taking the rest of its local and group amounts, so a line capitalised in full
// leaves the clearing account at zero in all three currencies. The value date defaults to the line's
// posting date and may lie in any period up to the end of the posting date's (422
// `assets.value_date_in_future` after it); the posting date defaults to the company's date today, and
// lies on or after the start of the asset's latest center assignment (409
// `assets.posting_before_center_transfer`). It posts in the line's currency, to every ledger the
// company keeps, Dr the class's acquisition cost account on the asset's profit center, Cr the clearing
// account on the line's profit center (document type AA, source context `assets`; 422
// `ledger.period_not_open` when the period is not open in a ledger), writes the asset's movements per
// ledger, and makes a `planned` asset `active` with its capitalisation date the earliest value date of
// its acquisitions that add cost. Against an offset account: `amount` is required and positive, in any
// currency; the offset account is a G/L account set up in the company, active and not blocked, that is
// no reconciliation account of any kind, not the GR/IR account and no asset clearing account of the
// company (422 `assets.offset_account_invalid`). It posts Dr the class's acquisition cost account on
// the asset's profit center / Cr the offset account, converted at the posting date's rate (422
// `ledger.no_exchange_rate` without one), to every ledger the company keeps; the asset's cost per
// ledger is the cost line's local and group amounts as the ledger posted them. It serves what has no
// supplier line (own work capitalised, a contribution in kind). Its value date defaults to its posting
// date; the date rules above apply. A cancelled or retired asset is 422 `assets.asset_cancelled` or
// `assets.asset_retired`; an asset the caller cannot act in is 404. Audited, and published as
// AssetMovementPosted.
//
// POST /v1/asset-acquisitions
func (UnimplementedHandler) CreateAssetAcquisition(ctx context.Context, req *AssetAcquisitionCreate, params CreateAssetAcquisitionParams) (r CreateAssetAcquisitionRes, _ error) {
	return r, ht.ErrNotImplemented
}

// CreateAssetRetirement implements createAssetRetirement operation.
//
// Retires a share of an active asset (ADR-0057 §8, T57g; SAP's retirement with revenue or scrapping):
// `kind` `sale` with its `proceeds` (required, zero or more, in the company's local currency) or
// `scrapping` (no proceeds). `share` is the share of cost retired, as a `percent` (above 0, at most
// 100, up to four decimals) or as a `cost` in local currency (at most the asset's cost: 422
// `assets.share_exceeds`), one of the two; without it, all of it (a full retirement). The posting date
// defaults to the company's date today and the value date to the posting date; a retirement is not
// backdated: its value date lies in its posting date's period, on or before the posting date (422
// `assets.value_date_outside_period`), and on or after the asset's capitalisation date (422
// `assets.value_date_before_capitalisation`), and the asset may have no movement or center assignment
// valued in a later period nor depreciation booked through a later period in any ledger, a
// depreciation run of a later period included, even one that posted nothing for it (409
// `assets.later_value_date`). Per ledger the company keeps (the leading ledger first, then by code) it
// first catches up the depreciation through the period before the posting period: the planned
// depreciation through that period less what is booked, negative when the posting period's run already
// posted (its depreciation is posted back: the share retired depreciates for none of its period); the
// asset is then booked through that period, unless it is already booked through the posting period. It
// then takes the share of the asset's balances in that ledger as posted so far, the catch-up included:
// the cost, accumulated depreciation and residual value, each rounded half away from zero, all of them
// for a full share; with no cost left in a ledger (a credit memo took it all) only a full share is
// accepted (422 `assets.share_of_zero_cost`). Each ledger gets one entry of its own (document type AA,
// source context `assets`): Dr depreciation / Cr accumulated depreciation for the catch-up (the other
// way round when negative), Dr accumulated depreciation for the share taken, Cr acquisition cost for
// the cost taken, Dr the sale clearing account for the proceeds, and the difference, Cr gain or Dr
// loss (the gain on the asset's profit center, the depreciation and loss on its cost center and profit
// center, the balance sheet lines on its profit center). In group currency the catch-up and the
// proceeds are translated at the posting date's rate (422 `ledger.no_exchange_rate` without one), the
// cost and accumulated depreciation taken carry their booked group amounts (all of them when nothing
// is left locally; the translated amount when the booked group amount has the other sign; else in
// proportion), and the gain or loss takes the group difference, so each entry balances in both
// currencies; a ledger in which every amount is zero gets no entry. Proceeds are the same in every
// ledger; the gain or loss differs as the book values do. A share that leaves no cost in every ledger,
// or a full one, retires the asset; the buyer's invoice and any tax on the sale are posted as a manual
// entry that clears the sale clearing account. A planned asset is 422 `assets.asset_not_capitalised`;
// a retired or cancelled one 422 `assets.asset_retired` or `assets.asset_cancelled`; an asset without
// terms in a ledger its company keeps 422 `assets.ledger_terms_missing`; an asset the caller cannot
// act in is 404. Audited, and published as AssetMovementPosted (and AssetChanged `retired` when it
// retires the asset). Human-only.
//
// POST /v1/asset-retirements
func (UnimplementedHandler) CreateAssetRetirement(ctx context.Context, req *AssetRetirementCreate, params CreateAssetRetirementParams) (r CreateAssetRetirementRes, _ error) {
	return r, ht.ErrNotImplemented
}

// CreateAssetTransfer implements createAssetTransfer operation.
//
// Transfers an asset (ADR-0057 §8, T57h; SAP's transfer within a company code). `kind` `center` moves
// the asset to another cost center (`cost_center_id`; its profit center is the cost center's on the
// value date, a different `profit_center_id` given is 422 `ledger.profit_center_mismatch`) or, for an
// asset without a cost center, to another profit center (`profit_center_id`); the centers are checked
// as a posting on the class's depreciation account on the value date would check them (422
// `ledger.cost_center_invalid`, `ledger.profit_center_invalid`, `assets.cost_center_required`), and
// must differ from the asset's (422 `request.validation_failed`). It is valued on the first day of its
// posting period (the default; another day of the period is 422 `assets.transfer_mid_period`, another
// period 422 `assets.value_date_outside_period`). Per ledger the company keeps (the leading ledger
// first, then by code) it first catches up the depreciation through the period before on the asset's
// previous centers, as a retirement does (negative when the posting period's run already posted), and
// books the asset through that period; the catch-up is an entry of its own (`part` `catch_up`) dated
// that period's last day when that period is open in the ledger, the asset is not booked through the
// transfer's period and its previous centers were already in force then; otherwise it is posted in the
// transfer's entry, on the previous centers, which must still be valid then. When the profit center
// changes, the transfer moves all the asset's booked cost and accumulated depreciation in each ledger,
// after the catch-up, at their local and group amounts from the previous profit center to the new one
// on the same accounts (`part` `main`). It writes no movement but the catch-up's; from then on every
// posting for the asset takes the new centers, a depreciation run of an earlier period passes over the
// asset and an acquisition is posted on or after the transfer's value date (409
// `assets.posting_before_center_transfer`). `kind` `asset` moves a share of the asset's balances
// (`share`, as for a retirement; default all of it) to another asset of the same company
// (`to_asset_id`; another company's is 422 `assets.transfer_other_company`, the asset itself 422
// `assets.transfer_same_asset`), possibly of another class (a reclassification): valued in its posting
// period on or before its posting date (default the posting date, 422
// `assets.value_date_outside_period`) and not before the asset's capitalisation date (422
// `assets.value_date_before_capitalisation`), posted on or after the receiver's latest center
// assignment (409 `assets.posting_before_center_transfer`), it catches up the asset's depreciation as
// a retirement does, then per ledger posts one entry: Cr the asset's acquisition cost and Dr the
// receiver's for the cost taken, Dr the asset's accumulated depreciation and Cr the receiver's for the
// accumulated depreciation taken, each on its asset's profit center, at their booked group amounts;
// the residual value taken goes to the receiver, which depreciates the net value received over its own
// remaining life. A planned receiver becomes active with the transfer's value date as its
// capitalisation date; a full transfer retires the asset. For either kind, the asset (and the
// receiver) may have no movement or center assignment valued in a later period nor depreciation booked
// through a later period in any ledger (409 `assets.later_value_date`); a planned asset is 422
// `assets.asset_not_capitalised` for an asset transfer; a retired or cancelled asset or receiver 422
// `assets.asset_retired` or `assets.asset_cancelled`; an asset without terms in a ledger its company
// keeps 422 `assets.ledger_terms_missing`; an asset or receiver the caller cannot act in is 404. A
// ledger with nothing to post gets no entry. Audited, and published as AssetMovementPosted (and
// AssetChanged `retired` when a full transfer retires the asset). Human-only.
//
// POST /v1/asset-transfers
func (UnimplementedHandler) CreateAssetTransfer(ctx context.Context, req *AssetTransferCreate, params CreateAssetTransferParams) (r CreateAssetTransferRes, _ error) {
	return r, ht.ErrNotImplemented
}

